<title>BELGIUM: EU divided on malting barley quotas.</title>
<headline>EU divided on malting barley quotas.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>A European Commission proposal to open a 30,000 tonne reduced tariff quota for imports of U.S. malting barley is unlikely to be agreed by farm ministers next week, an EU member state official said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;The proposal was discussed by the special agriculture committee on Tuesday. Twelve member states were opposed and it is hard to see agreement being reached in Council,&quot; the official said.</p>
<p>The European Commission is committed to opening the quota at a tariff reduced by 50 percent under a GATT agreement covering enlargement of the European Union.</p>
<p>The original proposal was blocked by an EU trade committee on December 20 on the grounds that the EU already has too much malting barley, used for brewing beer.</p>
<p>The United States has complained about the delay in opening the quota which would compensate its exporters for the loss of Austrian, Swedish and Finnish markets after these countries joined the EU in 1995. -- Brussels Newsroom 32 2 287 6800</p>
</text>
